{
  "dom_id": "step-q143473",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=3,\\,{x}_{2}=4,\\,{x}_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\theta +b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =3!\\cdot 4!\\cdot 2!=6\\cdot 24\\cdot 2=288, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =3+4+2=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-3\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡288.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-3+9+288=294.\n$$",
